{"type":"message","id":"e70d1ce1","parentId":"c809ab02","timestamp":"2026-10-17T22:49:09.872Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I changed `parse_date` in `dates/parse.py` to use `datetime.fromisoformat(s)` instead of the fixed `strptime` format. The old format had nothing to match a timezone, so the trailing `Z` caused the error. `tests/test_parse.py::test_dates` now passes, along with the other two tests in `tests/`.\n\nOne behaviour change to be aware of: timestamps that carry a timezone (`Z` or an offset like `+02:00`) now return a timezone-aware datetime, where before they failed outright. Timestamps without a timezone still return a plain datetime, as they did before. I checked both cases by hand.\n\n**Risk:** comparing or subtracting a timezone-aware datetime and a plain one raises `TypeError`. That only matters if callers mix the two types. `day_key` isn't affected because it only formats the date.\n\nThis needs Python 3.11 or newer, since older versions of `fromisoformat` reject `Z`. The environment here runs 3.13.5."}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"vertex-anthropic","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":328,"cacheRead":26001,"cacheWrite":608,"totalTokens":26939,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cttl":{"ephemeral5m":608}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1792277345814,"responseId":"msg_vrtx_011Cg8dE8Ws5jJT7VbvqUS2U","duration":4045.767211999999,"ttft":4027.723424,"contextSnapshot":{"promptTokens":26611,"nonMessageTokens":21589,"compactionEpoch":0}}}
